---
title: Interceptors
description: The pre, post, exception and final pipeline stages on the stable v1 line.
---

Interceptors attach cross-cutting concerns to a dispatch without handlers knowing about
them. They are registered exactly like handlers — `RegisterFromAssembly` finds them — and
run as part of the same pipeline.

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler and may rewrite the message or abort.
- **post** runs after a successful handler and may rewrite the result.
- **exception** runs when something threw, and may supply a result instead.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage: direct registrations first, then covariant ones, each ordered by `[Weight]`
descending.

## Pre-interceptors

Returns the message the next stage will see:

```csharp
public sealed class Authorize : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct>
{
    private readonly ICurrentUser _user;

    public Authorize(ICurrentUser user) => _user = user;

    public Task<object> HandleAsync(DeleteProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.Can("products:delete"))
            context.Abort();

        return Task.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Validation, authorization, logging and context enrichment all belong here. Throwing sends
control to the exception stage; `context.Abort()` ends the pipeline cleanly.

## Post-interceptors

Receives the result and returns what the caller gets:

```csharp
public sealed class AuditCreation : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public Task<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record("product.created", result);
        return Task.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Post-interceptors run **only on success** — not after a throw, not after an abort.

## Exception interceptors

Receives the message, whatever result existed, and the exception:

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound : IQueryExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    public Task<Product?> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, Product? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is KeyNotFoundException)
            return Task.FromResult<Product?>(null);

        throw exception;      // not ours — let it propagate
    }
}
```

Returning a value marks the exception handled. Rethrow what you do not own — a blanket
interceptor that swallows everything hides real bugs.

## Final interceptors

Always run, whatever happened, and cannot change anything:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public Task HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both `result` and `exception` are nullable because exactly one of them is meaningful on any
given run. This is the stage for cleanup, metrics and audit trails.

## Arities

Each stage comes in several forms, trading breadth for type safety:

| Form | Applies to |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | every command in the module |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | one command type or hierarchy |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | typed rewriting |

Post and exception interceptors additionally have a three-parameter
`<TCommand, TResult, TModifiedResult>` form.

{% aside type="note" %}
Those three-parameter interfaces are **deprecated since v1.4.0 and removed in v2**, where the
two-parameter typed interfaces expose the typed `HandleAsync` directly. Prefer the
two-parameter form in new code. See
[the migration guide](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v1-to-v2).
{% /aside %}

Queries and events offer the same stages as `IQueryPreInterceptor…` and
`IEventPreInterceptor…`. The [API reference](/ergosfare.docs/api) carries the exact signature
for every one.

## Ordering

`[Weight(n)]` orders interceptors within a stage, higher first:

```csharp
[Weight(200)] public sealed class Authorize : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Validate  : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(10)]  public sealed class Log       : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
```

Weight orders within a directness group — a high-weight interceptor registered against
`ICommand` still runs after an unweighted one registered against `DeleteProduct`.

`[Group("audit")]` scopes an interceptor to dispatches that ask for it:

```csharp
var settings = new CommandMediationSettings();
settings.Filters.Groups = ["audit"];

await commands.SendAsync(new DeleteProduct(id), settings);
```

An empty filter — the default — runs everything registered.

---

The [v2 interceptor documentation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors) covers
the same four stages in more depth, and adds `[ExcludeFromPipeline]` for keeping high-volume
messages out of covariantly matched interceptors.
